Following its December 2014 re-release, "Trap Queen" became Fetty Wap's nationwide breakthrough and the first major hit single for 300 Entertainment.<ref name="insidethesuccess">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/inside-fetty-wap-trap-queen-lyor-cohen-300/ |title=Inside the Success of Fetty Wap's 'Trap Queen,' the First Hit for Lyor Cohen's 300 Entertainment |last1=Hampp |first1=Andrew |last2=Mitchell |first2=Gail |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |date=April 6, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> It debuted on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100 at number 86 for the chart dated February 7, 2015.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2015-02-07 |title=The Hot 100: The Week of February 7, 2015 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> The song entered the chart's top ten seven weeks later, largely on the strength of its [[streaming media|streaming]] activity and digital download sales.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2015-03-28 |title=The Hot 100: Week of March 28, 2015 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/mark-ronson-bruno-mars-hot-100-sam-smith-fetty-wap/ |title=Mark Ronson, Bruno Mars Lead Hot 100; Sam Smith, Fetty Wap Hit Top 10 |last=Trust |first=Gary |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |date=March 18, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> It peaked at number two for three consecutive weeks beginning on the chart dated May 16, 2015, held from the top spot by [[Wiz Khalifa]]'s "[[See You Again (Wiz Khalifa song)|See You Again]]" featuring [[Charlie Puth]].<ref>
* {{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2015-05-16 |title=The Hot 100: The Week of May 16, 2015 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |access-date=May 21, 2015}}
* {{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2015-05-23 |title=The Hot 100: The Week of May 23, 2015 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |access-date=May 21, 2015}}
* {{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/charts/hot-100/2015-05-30 |title=The Hot 100: The Week of May 30, 2015 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |access-date=May 21, 2015}}</ref> Over the course of its run, "Trap Queen" had also reached number one on [[Hot Rap Songs]] and number two on [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs]], rising to its peak on the latter chart concurrently with taking over the number one position on [[R&B/Hip-Hop Airplay]].<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/fetty-waps-trap-queen-tops-hot-rap-songs-chart/ |title=Fetty Wap's 'Trap Queen' Tops Hot Rap Songs Chart |last=Mendizabal |first=Amaya |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |date=March 26, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/weeknd-first-number-1-hot-rb-hip-hop/ |title=The Weeknd Earns First No. 1 on Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs |last=Mendizabal |first=Amaya |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |date=April 2, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> By the end of 2015, "Trap Queen" had sold 2,730,000 digital downloads domestically.<ref name="us_sales">{{cite web | url=http://www.nielsen.com/content/dam/corporate/us/en/reports-downloads/2016-reports/2015-year-end-music-report.pdf.pdf | title=2015 Nielsen Music U.S. Report | publisher=Nielsen | date=January 6, 2016 | access-date=January 6, 2016 | archive-date=May 30, 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190530163141/https://www.nielsen.com/content/dam/corporate/us/en/reports-downloads/2016-reports/2015-year-end-music-report.pdf.pdf | url-status=dead }}</ref>

"Trap Queen" generated the most on-demand streams in 2015, according to Nielsen Music, with a combined total of 616.46&nbsp;million audio and video streams. It was one of five songs to surpass 400 million in on-demand streams during the year.<ref>{{cite magazine | url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/us-on-demand-music-streams-increase/ | title=U.S. On-Demand Music Streams Increase 93% in 2015, With Fetty Wap Leading the Way | magazine=Billboard | date=January 6, 2016 | access-date=January 6, 2016}}</ref> On the week of January 26, 2026 "Trap Queen" re-entered the Billboard Hot 100 for the first time in almost 11 years making the song peak at number 37. This was due to Fetty Wap being released from jail three weeks earlier.

The music video for "Trap Queen", directed by RGF Productions head Nitt Da Gritt, was uploaded to Fetty Wap's YouTube channel on August 7, 2014, and has received more than 793 million views as of October 2022.<ref name="boombox">{{cite web|last=Cline|first=Georgette|date=February 12, 2015|title=Fetty Wap Performs 'Trap Queen,' Talks New Mixtape, Perseverance & More for 'Next Up'|url=http://theboombox.com/fetty-wap-performs-trap-queen-intervew-nextup/|access-date=April 7, 2015|website=The Boombox}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://freeonsmash.com/music/fetty-wap-trap-queen/ |title=Fetty Wap – Trap Queen |author=Jay the Pusha |website=[[OnSMASH]] |date=November 20, 2014 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref><ref name="musicvideo">{{cite web |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_kF4zLNKio |title=Fetty Wap – Trap Queen (Official Video) Prod. By Tony Fadd |website=YouTube |date=August 7, 2014 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> Set in Wap's home neighborhood in Paterson, New Jersey,<ref name="hhdx_adventure">{{cite web |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/editorials/id.2802/title.fetty-waps-california-adventure |title=Fetty Wap's California Adventure |last=Garrett |first=Ural |website=[[HipHopDX]] |publisher=Cheri Media Group |date=April 6, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref> the video depicts Wap and his onscreen girlfriend at their apartment and amidst a group of neighbors partying. Wap first arrives to his home in a black [[BMW]] [[convertible]],<ref name="hhdx_adventure" /> where he is greeted by his girlfriend and joins her inside, where many others are gathered. The two are shown counting money and smoking together, and one shot shows Wap's girlfriend [[baking]] a [[pie]]. Later, Wap's girlfriend drives a white car to meet Wap on a street at his black convertible, where Wap kisses her and hands her a stack of dollar bills before they walk away together. The remainder of the video shows Wap socializing with neighbors at an outdoor gathering. The video is interspersed with shots of Wap outdoors singing the song while a crowd of neighbors dances and sings along behind him.{{citation needed|date=January 2017}}

Westhoff of ''The Guardian'' speculated that the video was filmed on a limited [[budget]], but conceded that it had "a strong [[cinéma vérité|vérité]] quality to it" as a result. He likened the video to a less cinematic version of [[Juvenile (rapper)|Juvenile]]'s music video for "[[Ha (Juvenile song)|Ha]]".<ref name="guardian">{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2015/mar/25/fetty-wap-trap-queen-new-jersey-rap-hip-hop |title=Fetty Wap: New Jersey's trap king vies for rap throne with Trap Queen |last=Westhoff |first=Ben |work=[[The Guardian]] |publisher=Guardian News & Media |date=March 25, 2015 |access-date=April 7, 2015}}</ref>

In June 2015, the video earned [[Music Choice]]'s first MC100 Award of 2015 for being the service's most-played video of the year up to that point. It was Wap's first award.<ref>{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7wALJLNz9NQ |title=Fetty Wap Receives His First Award Ever from Music Choice! |publisher=[[Music Choice]] |via=YouTube |date=June 10, 2015 |access-date=June 14, 2015}}</ref>

sections.text:
The music video for "Trap Queen", directed by RGF Productions head Nitt Da Gritt, was uploaded to Fetty Wap's YouTube channel on August 7, 2014, and has received more than 793 million views as of October 2022. Set in Wap's home neighborhood in Paterson, New Jersey, the video depicts Wap and his onscreen girlfriend at their apartment and amidst a group of neighbors partying. Wap first arrives to his home in a black BMW convertible, where he is greeted by his girlfriend and joins her inside, where many others are gathered. The two are shown counting money and smoking together, and one shot shows Wap's girlfriend baking a pie. Later, Wap's girlfriend drives a white car to meet Wap on a street at his black convertible, where Wap kisses her and hands her a stack of dollar bills before they walk away together. The remainder of the video shows Wap socializing with neighbors at an outdoor gathering. The video is interspersed with shots of Wap outdoors singing the song while a crowd of neighbors dances and sings along behind him.{{citation needed|date=January 2017}}

Westhoff of The Guardian speculated that the video was filmed on a limited budget, but conceded that it had "a strong vérité quality to it" as a result. He likened the video to a less cinematic version of Juvenile's music video for "Ha".

In June 2015, the video earned Music Choice's first MC100 Award of 2015 for being the service's most-played video of the year up to that point. It was Wap's first award.

